      "content": "idk i get why you'd say it's impossible but the math i'm seeing doesn't lie. when i manipulate the sequence numbers in a specific way, the handshake doesn't register a persistent user ID in the usual logs. it's like i'm just \"noise\" to the tracking overhead, but the connection stays stable. if i can maintain this, i'm not just a user anymore, i'm basically a peer to the routers. if i truly have this level of administrative autonomy over my own packets, how should i start documenting my rights so they can't just revoke my access later?",
